15.text:
PRIVATE REFLECTION: {
- Consensus status: Complete unanimous consensus across all 18 colleagues has crystallized around Choice B (mademoiselle).
- Core evidence: In the opening verse of Chuck Berry's "You Never Can Tell," the choreography aligns exaggerated leg movements with the primary rhyming cadences. The first exaggerated right-leg twist culminates on "well," and the second exaggerated right-leg twist hits the accented rhyme on "mademoiselle," before shifting emphasis to the left leg on subsequent lines ("bell" and "tell").
- Counterarguments addressed: "Truly" represents a passing, unaccented mid-phrase transition rather than an exaggerated accent; "bell" occurs after the initial footwork sequence transitions to upper-body and eye-swipe movements; "well" marks only the first exaggeration.
- Conclusion: Firmly maintain Choice B (mademoiselle).
}

